<%# WHAT THIS PAGE IS NOT TELLING YOU — stated on the page, at the top, before the
    galleries.

    THIS BLOCK IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE SURFACE. The operator is being asked to
    calibrate his taste against the machine's, and a calibration done against a
    flattering picture of the machine is worse than none: he would tune his judgement
    to a ranker that does not behave the way the page implied. Every known defect in
    this lane is therefore printed here, with what was measured and when.

    THE TWO DEFECTS ARE NOW GUARDED, AND THE PANEL SAYS SO RATHER THAN DISAPPEARING.
    `reference-photos-wrong-person` shipped the guards; a calibration page that simply
    deleted the warnings would leave the operator unable to tell a defect that was fixed
    from one nobody found. Each bullet now names the measurement, the guard, and what the
    guard still cannot catch — which is the part that matters when he is judging picks. %>
<div data-test="scouting-honesty"
     class="rounded-lg border border-warning/40 bg-warning/10 p-3 sm:p-4 space-y-3">
  <p class="text-xs font-black text-warning-ink uppercase tracking-wide">
    Read this before you judge the picks
  </p>

  <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-xs text-warning-ink">
    <li class="flex gap-2">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">&#9679;</span>
      <span>
        <strong>What actually ranked these:</strong>
        <% if @face_sized %>
          <%# THE STRONGEST STATE, and it is new: the classifier reported how much of the %>
          <%# frame each head fills, which is the measurement four real mints turned on. %>
          <span data-test="ranked-by-size">
            a vision classifier measured how much of the frame each face fills, and that
            &mdash; not how clearly the face shows &mdash; is what ordered this gallery.
            Face <em>size</em> is the variable the mint measurements turned on.
          </span>
        <% elsif @face_ranked %>
          <span data-test="ranked-by-visibility">
            a vision classifier judged how clearly each face shows, and reported NO face
            size. That is <em>visibility</em> only, so these are ordered by the weaker of
            the two signals and nothing here may go to Higgsfield&rsquo;s trainer.
          </span>
        <% else %>
          <strong>nothing looked at these photographs.</strong> They are ordered on
          shape and relevance only. A high position here means &ldquo;worth a closer
          look&rdquo;, never &ldquo;a face is visible&rdquo; &mdash; and because nothing
          examined them, <strong>none of them is picked</strong>: a photograph nobody has
          looked at cannot be shown to hold one person&rsquo;s face, so both generators
          fall back on the cached headshot. An empty picks section here is that refusal,
          not a failed search.
          <% if @face_ranking_available %>
            <%# CONFIGURED BUT SILENT, and this is the state that misled the operator %>
            <%# on 2026-09-26: the classifier answered nothing, and the page said only %>
            <%# that nothing looked, which reads as "no credential". Carries its own %>
            <%# data-test so the assertion is on STRUCTURE rather than on this prose. %>
            <span data-test="classifier-silent">
              The classifier IS configured here, so it either found nothing it could
              read or could not fetch these images &mdash; not the same thing as
              having no classifier at all.
              <% if admin? %>
                <%# ADMINS ONLY. /error_logs is behind require_admin, and a link that %>
                <%# bounces the reader to a sign-in wall is worse than no link. %>
                Check <%= link_to "the error log", error_logs_path, class: "underline" %>
                before trusting this order.
              <% else %>
                An admin can read why in the error log.
              <% end %>
            </span>
          <% else %>
            <span data-test="classifier-absent">
              The classifier needs
              <span class="font-mono"><%= Appearances::FaceVisibility::API_KEY_ENV %></span>
              and this machine has no such credential.
            </span>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      </span>
    </li>

    <li class="flex gap-2" data-test="defect-wrong-person">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">&#9679;</span>
      <span>
        <strong>A clear photo of the wrong man used to beat a helmeted photo of the right
        one.</strong> The classifier was asked whether a face is visible, and it answers
        that correctly for a stranger. Measured 2026-09-25: searching &ldquo;Drew
        Lock&rdquo; returned <span class="font-mono">Drew Hutton.jpg</span>, face 90,
        ranked second in the identity. A photograph whose title names somebody else is
        now refused outright and never paid to be classified.
        <strong>The title check is a filter, not the answer:</strong> it fires on a name
        that CONFLICTS with this person&rsquo;s &mdash; a shared given name with a
        different surname &mdash; so an untitled photograph, a mis-titled one, or a
        stranger who shares no name word at all still gets through.
        <strong>Read the titles.</strong>
      </span>
    </li>

    <li class="flex gap-2" data-test="defect-cannot-mint">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">&#9679;</span>
      <span>
        <strong>Two generators, and they do not take the same photographs.</strong>
        Four real mints on 2026-09-25: three wide action shots failed at prepare, the
        same three at full resolution failed, a bare-faced 556x780 sideline shot failed,
        and a tight ESPN headshot completed. Resolution is not the variable and the
        helmet is not the variable &mdash; face size in frame is. So the
        <strong>character sheet</strong> (zero-shot, no training step to refuse
        anything) is given every photograph below that passes the checks, while
        <strong>Higgsfield&rsquo;s trainer</strong> is only offered the ones whose face
        size something actually measured &mdash; at least
        <%= (Appearances::ReferenceEligibility::MINT_FACE_FILL * 100).round %>% of the
        frame. On a machine with no vision credential that is none of them, and the
        trainer gets the cached headshot alone. Tiles carry the evidence, never a
        prediction: no photograph has ever been minted between the failing anchors and
        the proven headshot.
      </span>
    </li>

    <% if @search_provider == Appearances::ImageSearch::WikimediaCommons.provider_name %>
      <li class="flex gap-2" data-test="commons-and-note">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">&#9679;</span>
        <span>
          <strong>The team in the query can collapse this search.</strong> The queries
          above are the person plus their team plus one variant, which helps a general
          image search tell a quarterback from a locksmith. Wikimedia Commons ANDs the
          terms instead, so the team narrows rather than disambiguates. Measured
          2026-09-26: &ldquo;Drew Lock&rdquo; returns 20 candidates and
          &ldquo;Drew Lock Seattle Seahawks&rdquo; returns <strong>2</strong>, while
          &ldquo;Patrick Mahomes&rdquo; returns 20 either way. Precision goes up and
          recall goes down, and a 2-candidate answer cannot fill a cap of
          <%= @chosen_limit %>. If this page shows very few candidates, suspect the
          query before the archive &mdash; and note that a variant narrows it a second
          time on top of the team.
        </span>
      </li>
    <% end %>

    <%# THE FAN-OUT, ITS EVIDENCE, AND ITS LIMIT — the three facts an operator needs to
        read this page's candidate count without over-reading it. It is FIRST among the
        new notes because it is what changed. %>
    <li class="flex gap-2" data-test="fan-out-note">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">&#9679;</span>
      <span>
        <strong>Four searches, not one, and they really do differ.</strong> Each query
        above is bought separately &mdash; providers bill per query, not per result
        &mdash; and duplicates are dropped by image URL before anything is classified,
        so nothing is paid for twice. Measured against live Serper on 2026-09-27:
        <strong>107 unique of 120</strong> across six variants for one athlete, and
        <strong>73 of 80</strong> across these four for another. What did NOT hold across
        the two is which variant wins: &ldquo;no helmet&rdquo; returned 11 tall
        candidates of 20 against 3 for the bare name on the first athlete and only 8
        against 6 on the second. So the variants are worth their queries; a ranking of
        the variants is not something this page can yet claim, which is what the
        breakdown below the galleries is for.
      </span>
    </li>

    <li class="flex gap-2" data-test="vision-ceiling-note">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">&#9679;</span>
      <span>
        <strong>Only <%= @vision_shortlist %> of the candidates are ever looked at.</strong>
        Four searches return roughly 72 unique photographs and the face classifier is
        paid for <span class="font-mono">VISION_SHORTLIST = <%= @vision_shortlist %></span>
        of them &mdash; about a third &mdash; because it bills per image while a search
        bills per query. The shortlist is filled round by round across the four searches
        rather than by taking the top <%= @vision_shortlist %> overall, so a variant
        cannot be shut out by another variant's bigger, cleaner action shots; that is what
        keeps the expressions you asked for in the running. Everything past the shortlist
        is filed with a reason and <em>nobody looked at it</em>, which is why it reads
        <span class="font-mono">face_unscored</span> rather than as a judgement.
      </span>
    </li>

    <li class="flex gap-2" data-test="chosen-limit-note">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="shrink-0">&#9679;</span>
      <span>
        <strong>The cap is <%= @chosen_limit %>, not 5.</strong> You asked for the five
        best; the pipeline shipped with six and the fan-out raised it to
        <span class="font-mono">CHOSEN_LIMIT = <%= @chosen_limit %></span>, and this page
        reports what it actually does. Everything past the cap is filed with a reason
        rather than dropped, so changing the number is a re-pick and not another search
        &mdash; which is exactly why it is the cheap ceiling to move and the one above is
        not.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>
